<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaykh Junayd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1460">1460</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Junayd of Ardabil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaykh Junayd was head of the Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> from 1447 to 1460. Driven from Ardabil by a rival, he turned the order into a militant movement, won followers among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and married a sister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>. He was killed in battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> in 1460; his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> founded the Safavid state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd.jpg" alt="Shaykh Junayd" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gulustan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:02252_020qusar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Junayd was the son of Shaykh Ibrahim and the grandson of Khvajeh Ali Safavi, hereditary leaders of the Safavid order, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He took over the leadership of the order on his father's death in 1447. Until then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safaviyya">Safaviyya</a> had been organised around a saint and ascetic; under Junayd it became an active military movement with a policy of conquest, aimed at winning him a principality of his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Junayd was the first Safavid spiritual leader to espouse specifically Shia teachings, in particular those of the Twelver ghulat, and his followers regarded him as a divine incarnation. He drew so many disciples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> that in 1448 Jahan Shah, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu">Kara Koyunlu</a> prince, drove him into exile. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he carried on missionary work and gathered a following among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a>.</p><p>He then went to the court of Uzun Hassan at Diyarbakır, where, at some point between 1456 and 1459, he married Uzun Hassan's sister, Khadija Khatun. Barred from returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, he lived in Shirvan, and there he was killed on 4 March 1460 in a local skirmish near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samur-river">Samur River</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Junayd was buried where he fell, and his mausoleum stands in the village of Khazra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. His death began a lasting hostility between the mainly Sunni Shirvanshah and the increasingly heterodox Shia Safaviyya. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a>, who married his cousin Halima Alamshah Khatun, daughter of Uzun Hassan and Theodora Despina Khatun; they had three sons and three daughters. One of their sons was Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> and was the father of Shah Tahmasp I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shaykh Junayd” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruling house was Sunni Muslim, but it showed favour to Sufi orders, and Uzun Hasan married his sister to <b>Shaykh Junayd</b> and his daughter to Junayd’s son Haydar, leaders of the Safavid order of Ardabil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Shia Islam spread in their lands through <b>Shaykh Junayd</b> of the Safavid order and the Musha'sha' in Khuzistan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1460 <b>Shaykh Junayd</b>, the order's militant leader, was killed in battle with the forces of Khalilullah I, and in 1488 his son Shaykh Haydar was killed by Farrukh Yasar, who was aided by the Aq Qoyunlu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.25,48.3&amp;year=1450" aria-label="Ardabil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5752 790 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shaykh Junayd"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6202 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6202 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.25,48.3&amp;year=1450">What was here in 1450? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
